Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 0:27:11 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: PPL: Many Types of Intellectual Property Are Not Protected

It's been a while since I last had the chance to debate Hal Finney...it
feels good!

Hal writes:

> Tim makes the good point that there are many areas where a lack of
> intellectual property protection has not stopped innovation, that
> doesn't really prove anything.  Perhaps there could be even more
> innovation if intellectual property were protected in these fields.
> What if Warhol got a royalty for each piece of modern art?  That many
> more people would produce dazzling and original art, because it would
> be that much easier to get rich.

Maybe. Who knows? Perhaps Warhol would've gotten rich(er), but those
that followed would have had a much harder time, what with hiring
lawyers to make sure they didn' "infringe," paying Warhol the
royalties (set by whom, and at what rate?), and so on.

> The problem with having no patents is that you can invent something, some
> major improvement in an industrial process, or a startling new gadget
> (like the zipper), or whatever.  But you can't profit from it because
> your competitors are able to copy what you've done.

Some personal experience. I worked for Intel from 1974 to 1986, as
I've written about before. In its first 10 years of existence,
beginning in 1968, Intel invented/pioneered/marketed the MOS RAM, the
silicon gate process, the microprocessor, the EPROM, the single board
computer, the software development system, and even "DOS" (they hired
Gary Kildall to write an OS for Intel micros...this became CP/M, which
became the prototype for MS-DOS).

Almost nothing was patented. In those days, patents were largely seen
as worthless, being automatically cross-licensed (practically by
government decree...this was the time of "avoiding the appearance of
monopolistic action"). Intel's protection was in keeping things
secret. The silicon gate process was known only to Intel for several
years, as Mostek, T.I. , Fairchild, and National all tried to make
their version work. (The effect of alpha particles on memories, which
I discovered, was a deep, dark secret within Intel from my discovery
of the effect in early 1977 to our release of the details more than a
year later. We _knew_ that Mostek was being practically destroyed by
their "soft error" problem in computers and point of sale terminals,
but we said nothing. I mention this to convey the importance of
corporate secrets.)

And yet without patents, Intel prospered. It stayed ahead of the
competition (whatever you may think of the 8086!), and it filed for
very few patents.

> Look at what happens in the drug business.  I am constantly reading that
> drug companies won't test various natural substances because they are not
> patentable, hence there is no opportunity to make profits to cover the
> expensive testing phase.  (Granted, governments may make testing more
> expensive than necessary, but I'm sure we all would prefer to buy well-tested
> drugs even under PPL.)  Will anyone here dispute that removing patent
> protection would slow the rate of new drug creation?

Expensive, multi-year tests are of course largely a result of
overcautious, ass-covering by the FDA. It may be true that companies
that launch 10-year product development hoop-jumping cycles then want
the government to reward them with monopolies (even over independent
discoveries, one of the foulest implications of patents), but this
should not be construed as a justification for patents.

> How much funding would a biotech startup get if it announced a policy
> of not seeking patent protection for its discoveries?  They'd never get
> off the ground.  There are just a lot of areas like this which are
> expensive to enter but for which the products are cheap to produce and
> easy to copy.  In those areas, patent protection will greatly speed the
> rate of innovation and discovery.

Perhaps Hal is right, perhaps not. I again cite the many areas where
government-granted concessions are not the norm, as in my post
earlier, and suggest that even biotech will make advances without
patents. Actually, from my understanding of biotech, the _route_ to a
molecule, the production process, is the hard part...and this is the
part that may in fact be kept secret. Merely having a vial of
WonderDrug 37 is not enough to be able to make it, any more than
Intel's competitors in 1975 could look at Intel's NMOS memories and
figure out out to make them.

-Tim


