Date: Tue, 26 Jan 1993 00:28:54 -0800
From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: PPL's and justice

>> But suppose some PPLs *do* enforce contracts signed by seven year
>> olds?  Would that be an injustice?
 
>> Eli Brandt
 
> Depends on whom you ask.  I would rather not raise my kids under
> such a system.  Or a variety of other regimes -- the ones allowing
> random slayings, execution without trial, witch burnings, and so
> forth.  Given that no legal system in the world, as far as I know,
> considers 7-year-olds of age, while some do provide for execution
> without trial, I'm not sure it's worth worrying about.
 
> Dale Worley	
 
Dale and Eli are addressing the pivotal question.  The reason I'm not a fan
of natural law per se is that it necessarily reflects the values of the
society from which it is drawn.  If that society is coercive, its natural
law will be anti-libertarian.  I agree with the argument that even in such a
society natural law will be less oppressive than State law, but it will
still be more oppressive than what I consider to be legitimate law.  
 
Mark Read Pickens   autarch@well.sf.ca.us

